    answer: 'Muslims have two festivals in the year, and both are days of worship, joy and giving.\n\n- Eid al-Fitr falls on the 1st of Shawwal and ends the month of fasting. Zakat al-fitr is given before the Eid prayer so the poor can celebrate as well.\n- Eid al-Adha falls on the 10th of Dhul Hijjah, after the day of Arafah in Hajj. It remembers Ibrahim\'s obedience, and those who can afford it offer a sacrifice. Allah says the sacrifice is about devotion, not the meat: "Their meat will not reach Allah, nor will their blood, but what reaches Him is piety from you" (Qur\'an 22:37).\n\nBoth days begin with the Eid prayer in congregation. On Eid al-Adha the Prophet ﷺ taught that the prayer comes first and the sacrifice after it (Sahih al-Bukhari 5545).\n\nFasting is not allowed on these two days: Umar said the Prophet forbade fasting on the day you break your fast and the day you eat the meat of your sacrifices (Sahih al-Bukhari 1990).\n\nIslam has no other festivals for the community, and the Prophet ﷺ said there is an Eid for every people (Sahih al-Bukhari 952). Ask me how to pray the Eid prayer, about zakat al-fitr, or about Ibrahim\'s story.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 22:37; Sahih al-Bukhari 5545, 1990, 952.\n[GO:/calendar]',
