    answer: 'Iddah is the waiting period a woman keeps after a divorce or the death of her husband. The Qur\'an sets it:\n- Divorce, for a woman who has her periods: "Divorced women remain in waiting for three periods" (Qur\'an 2:228).\n- Divorce, for one who no longer has periods or has not had them: "their period is three months" (Qur\'an 65:4).\n- Pregnancy, whether after a divorce or a death: "for those who are pregnant, their term is until they give birth" (Qur\'an 65:4).\n- A widow: "wait four months and ten [days]" (Qur\'an 2:234).\n- Divorce before the marriage was consummated: "divorce them before you have touched them, then there is not for you any waiting period to count concerning them" (Qur\'an 33:49).\n\nDuring the iddah of a divorce the Qur\'an tells the husband not to put her out of her home (Qur\'an 65:1). The details of what a widow may and may not do, and how to count the days in your case, differ in practice; ask a qualified scholar about your own situation.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:228, 65:4, 2:234, 33:49, 65:1.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-nikah]',
