  CoreEntry(
    id: 'tashahhud-salawat',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\btashahhud\b',
      r'\bat ?tahiyyat\b',
      r'\btahiyyat\b',
      r'\blast sitting\b',
      r'\bfinal sitting\b',
      r'\bwhat (?:do i|should i|to) (?:say|recite|read) (?:when|in|during) (?:the )?(?:sitting|jalsa|qaada)\b',
      r'\bsalawat\b',
      r'\bdurood\b',
      r'\bdarood\b',
      r'\bsalat ?\w* ibrahim\b',
      r'\bibrahimiyya\b',
      r'\bsend(?:ing)? blessings? on the prophet\b',
      r'\bwhat (?:do i|should i) say after tashahhud\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:virtue|reward|benefit|friday)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'The tashahhud is said sitting in the second rak\'ah of prayers of three or four rak\'ahs (the short form), and in the last sitting of every prayer. The Prophet ﷺ taught Ibn Mas\'ud its words (Sahih al-Bukhari 831):\n\nالتَّحِيَّاتُ لِلَّهِ، وَالصَّلَوَاتُ وَالطَّيِّبَاتُ، السَّلاَمُ عَلَيْكَ أَيُّهَا النَّبِيُّ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ، السَّلاَمُ عَلَيْنَا وَعَلَى عِبَادِ اللَّهِ الصَّالِحِينَ‏ أَشْهَدُ أَنْ لاَ إِلَهَ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ، وَأَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُهُ وَرَسُولُهُ\n\nAt-tahiyyatu lillahi was-salawatu wat-tayyibat. As-salamu \'alayka ayyuhan-nabiyyu wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuh. As-salamu \'alayna wa \'ala \'ibadillahis-salihin. Ash-hadu an la ilaha illallah, wa ash-hadu anna Muhammadan \'abduhu wa rasuluh.\n\nMeaning: all greetings, prayers and good things are for Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and messenger.\n\nIn the last sitting, after the tashahhud, you send blessings on the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ taught his companions to say (Sahih al-Bukhari 3370):\n\nاللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى مُحَمَّدٍ، وَعَلَى آلِ مُحَمَّدٍ، كَمَا صَلَّيْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ، إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ اللَّهُمَّ بَارِكْ عَلَى مُحَمَّدٍ، وَعَلَى آلِ مُحَمَّدٍ، كَمَا بَارَكْتَ عَلَى إِبْرَاهِيمَ، وَعَلَى آلِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ، إِنَّكَ حَمِيدٌ مَجِيدٌ\n\nAllahumma salli \'ala Muhammadin wa \'ala ali Muhammad, kama sallayta \'ala Ibrahima wa \'ala ali Ibrahim, innaka hamidun majid. Allahumma barik \'ala Muhammadin wa \'ala ali Muhammad, kama barakta \'ala Ibrahima wa \'ala ali Ibrahim, innaka hamidun majid.\n\nThen you may ask Allah for whatever you wish before the salam. In the second sitting of a three- or four-rak\'ah prayer, the schools differ on whether to stop at the tashahhud or add the blessings: ask your imam.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 831, 3370.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-prayer]',
    topic: 'tashahhud',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The transliteration is typed by hand from the Arabic shown; please have a reader check the transliteration. The practice of the second sitting differs by school.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'sujud-sahw',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bsujud (?:as |al |of )?sahw\b',
      r'\bsujud sahu\b',
      r'\bsajdah sahw\b',
      r'\bforgetfulness prostration\b',
      r'\bprostration of forgetfulness\b',
      r'\bforgot\b.*\b(?:rakah|tashahhud|sujud|ruku|sit)\b',
      r'\bforget\b.*\b(?:rakah|tashahhud)\b',
      r'\b(?:lost count|not sure|unsure|doubt)\b.*\brakah\b',
      r'\bmistake (?:in|during) (?:the )?prayers?\b',
      r'\badded (?:an )?extra rakah\b',
      r'\bprayed (?:an )?extra\b',
      r'\bsahw\b',
      r'\bmade a mistake\b.*\bprayers?\b',
      r'\bcounted wrong\b',
      r'\bhow (?:do|to) (?:i )?fix.*mistake.*prayer\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Sujud as-sahw (the prostration of forgetfulness) is two extra prostrations you make when you forget something in the prayer: you add a rak\'ah or movement, leave out a required act (like the middle sitting), or are unsure how many rak\'ahs you prayed. After them you sit and give the salam.\n\nWhen you make it differs between the schools:\n- Hanafi: after the salam (one salam, then the two prostrations, then tashahhud and the final salam).\n- Shafi\'i: before the final salam.\n- Maliki: before the salam when something was left out, after it when something was added.\n- Hanbali: follows the hadiths, so before the salam in most cases, after it where the Prophet ﷺ did so.\n\nBoth ways are in the Sunnah. When the Prophet ﷺ got up from the middle sitting without sitting, he made two prostrations before the salam (Sahih al-Bukhari 829). When he gave the salam after two rak\'ahs by mistake, then completed the prayer, he made two prostrations after the salam (Sahih al-Bukhari 1229).\n\nIf you are unsure how many rak\'ahs you prayed, the Prophet ﷺ said to put the doubt aside, build on what you are sure of and make two prostrations (Sahih Muslim 571). The sujud does not repair a pillar you left out; that must be prayed again.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 829, 1229; Sahih Muslim 571.',
    topic: 'sujud sahw',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Per-school timing of the prostrations is standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
