    answer: 'Ghusl is the full washing of the body that you must do after janabah (major impurity). Allah says: "if you are in a state of janabah, then purify yourselves" (Qur\'an 5:6), and Qur\'an 4:43.\n\nWhen it is required:\n- After sexual intercourse (even without emission).\n- After an emission of semen, awake or in a dream. A woman who has a wet dream does ghusl if she sees a discharge (Sahih al-Bukhari 282). If you wake up and see no wetness, ghusl is not required.\n- When menstruation ends, and when postnatal bleeding (nifas) ends.\n\nHow to do it (the Prophet\'s way): Aisha described the Prophet\'s bath after janabah: he washed his hands, made wudu as for prayer, ran wet fingers through the roots of his hair, poured three handfuls of water on his head, and then poured water over the rest of his body (Sahih al-Bukhari 248).\n1. Intend in your heart and say "Bismillah".\n2. Wash your hands and private parts, and remove any impurity from the body.\n3. Make wudu as for prayer.\n4. Pour water over your head three times, reaching the roots of the hair.\n5. Pour water over your right side, then your left, so that water reaches the whole body, including the mouth, nose and under the nails.\n\nThe obligatory minimum: wash the whole body so that no spot, including the mouth and nose, stays dry, with the intention (the Hanafi school counts rinsing the mouth and nose as obligatory and the intention as a sunnah; the Shafi\'i school requires the intention). A woman need not undo her braids if water reaches the roots of her hair.\n\nA complete ghusl, with the mouth and nose rinsed, is enough to pray after: most scholars say it does the job of wudu too. The Prophet ﷺ\'s own practice, shown above, was to make wudu within it.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 5:6, 4:43; Sahih al-Bukhari 282, 248.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-ghusl]',
    topic: 'ghusl',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Which acts are obligatory in ghusl per school, braids, and whether a single ghusl replaces wudu are standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'tayammum',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\btayammum\b',
      r'\bdry ablution\b',
      r'\bpurif\w* with (?:earth|dust|soil|sand)\b',
      r'\b(?:no|without|cannot find|cant find|lack of|run out of) water\b.*\b(?:pray|prayer|wudu|ghusl|purif\w*)\b',
      r'\b(?:pray|prayer|wudu)\b.*\b(?:no|without) water\b',
      r'\bwater is not available\b',
      r'\bcan i pray without wudu\b',
      r'\bwhen is it allowed to skip wudu\b',
      r'\bcannot use water\b',
      r'\bcant use water\b',
      r'\bwater harms\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Tayammum is the dry purification: using clean earth in place of wudu or ghusl when you cannot use water. Allah says: "if you are ill or on a journey or one of you comes from the place of relieving himself or you have contacted women and do not find water, then seek clean earth and wipe over your faces and hands with it" (Qur\'an 5:6).\n\nWhen it is allowed:\n- There is no water within reach, and you have looked for it (or it is clearly not there).\n- Using water would harm you: an illness, a wound, or a danger that cold water would make it worse.\n- The water you have is needed for drinking, for yourself or others.\n\nHow to do it: the Prophet ﷺ taught \'Ammar ibn Yasir to strike the earth with his palms, blow into them, and wipe the face and the hands with them (Sahih al-Bukhari 338).\n1. Intend and say "Bismillah".\n2. Strike the clean earth (soil, sand, dust, or a clean stone with dust) once with both palms and blow off the excess.\n3. Wipe your face with your hands, then wipe the backs of your hands.\n\nThe schools differ on the details: the Hanafi and Shafi\'i schools say strike twice, wiping the face with the first and the arms to the elbows with the second. The Maliki school says two strikes too, with the hands to the wrists obligatory and to the elbows recommended. The Hanbali school, following the hadith above, says one strike and wipe the face and the hands to the wrists. Either way the tayammum takes the place of wudu or ghusl. It breaks like wudu does, and when water becomes available. The Prophet ﷺ said the earth has been made a place of prayer and a means of purification for his Ummah (Sahih al-Bukhari 335).\n\nSource: Qur\'an 5:6; Sahih al-Bukhari 338, 335.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-tayammum]',
    topic: 'tayammum',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The one-strike versus two-strike and elbows versus wrists difference per school, and the list of situations, are standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'najasa',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bnajasa\b',
      r'\btypes? of (?:impurity|impurities|najasa|filth)\b',
      r'\bimpurit(?:y|ies)\b',
      r'\bwhat (?:is|are) (?:najis|najasa|impure|unclean|filthy)\b',
      r'\bis (?:it|this|my) (?:najis|clean|pure|impure|najasa)\b',
      r'\bhow (?:to|do i) (?:clean|purify|remove) (?:najasa|impurity|urine|blood)\b',
      r'\bare (?:dogs?|cats?|pigs?|blood|semen|urine|vomit|alcohol|wine|saliva) (?:najis|impure|pure|clean|filthy)\b',
      r'\b(?:cat|dog)s?\b.*\b(?:impure|najasa|najis|clean|unclean)\b',
      r'\bwhat things are najis\b',
      r'\b(?:urine|blood|semen|madhy|mazi|wadi|vomit)\b.*\b(?:on|in) (?:my )?(?:clothes?|body|prayer|floor|mat)\b',
      r'\bclothes?\b.*\b(?:urine|blood|najis|impure)\b',
      r'\bmadhy\b',
      r'\bmazi\b',
      r'\bwadi\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Najasa is physical impurity that must be removed from your body, clothes and place of prayer before you pray. It is different from the impurity of janabah or hadath, which wudu and ghusl remove.\n\nThings that most scholars hold to be najis (impure):\n- Urine and stool of humans, and the thin fluid madhy and thick fluid wadi (washing the private part and the clothes it touched).\n- Flowing blood, and menstrual blood.\n- Dead animals (not killed by proper slaughter), except fish and locusts, and the flesh of swine (Qur\'an 5:3).\n\nThe schools differ on:\n- Semen: najis in the Hanafi and Maliki schools; pure in the Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools (though wiped away as a courtesy).\n- Alcohol: najis in most views, though some modern scholars say its impurity is spiritual, not physical.\n- A dog: the Prophet ﷺ said that when a dog drinks from your vessel, wash it seven times (Sahih al-Bukhari 172). The Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools take the dog\'s saliva as najis and wash seven times; the Maliki school takes the dog as pure and the washing as a ruling of worship; the Hanafi school takes the saliva as najis but differs on the number of washes. Cats are not unclean: the Prophet ﷺ said of a cat that it is not unclean, it is one of those that go round among you (Sunan Abu Dawud 75, hasan sahih per al-Albani).\n\nHow to remove it: wash with clean water until the colour, smell and taste of the najasa are gone. If something had been spilled in a masjid, the Prophet ﷺ told them to pour a bucket of water over it (Sahih al-Bukhari 220). A small amount that is hard to avoid is excused, within the limits each school sets.\n\nIf you are unsure whether something is najis, the default is that it is clean. Do not make yourself anxious over doubts.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 5:3; Sahih al-Bukhari 172, 220; Sunan Abu Dawud 75, hasan sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/huda]',
    topic: 'najasa',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The list of najasat, and the semen, alcohol and dog positions per school, are standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'menstruation',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bmenstrua\w*\b',
      r'\bhaid\w*\b',
      r'\bhayd\b',
      r'\bmonthly (?:cycle|bleeding|course)\b',
      r'\bmenses\b',
      r'\bnifas\b',
      r'\bpostnatal bleeding\b',
      r'\bpostpartum bleeding\b',
      r'\bistihadah?\b',
      r'\bmenopause\b',
      r'^(?=.*\b(?:my|her|the|a|during|on|while|with|have|having|got|get|when|if)\b.{0,12}\bperiods?\b)(?=.*\b(?:pray\w*|fast\w*|ramadan|quran|masjid|hajj|umrah|tawaf|husband|wife|woman|women|girl|girls|intimacy|sex|bath|ghusl|clean|pure|impure|miss\w*|make up|touch|read|recite|enter|allowed|permissible|can|cant|cannot|should|must|do|does|ends?|stopp?ed?)\b)',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:history|era|prophets time|time of the|years|decade|century|grace period|waiting period|iddah|probation|trial period|notice period|free period|school period|class period)\b',
      r'\b(?:game|story|stories|app|screen)\b',
      r'\b(?:pill|pills|delay|postpone|medicine|tablet|injection)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Menstruation (haid) is a natural state, not a sin or a shame, and Allah has made special allowances in it.\n\nWhile a woman has her period:\n- She does not pray, and she does not make those prayers up afterwards. A woman asked A\'ishah about it and she said, in the time of the Prophet ﷺ they were not ordered to make up the prayers they had missed (Sahih Muslim 335).\n- She does not fast, but she does make up the missed days of Ramadan later (Sahih al-Bukhari 1951).\n- She does not do tawaf of the Kaaba.\n- Intercourse is not allowed until she is clean and has bathed. Allah says: "keep away from wives during menstruation. And do not approach them until they are pure" (Qur\'an 2:222). Everything else with her husband is allowed in the schools\' view.\n- The Qur\'an: most scholars say she should not touch the mushaf directly (use a cloth or a phone). Reciting it is a school matter: the Hanafi, Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools restrict it, while the Maliki school and a number of other scholars allow it, especially for a student, a teacher or anyone who fears forgetting. The report usually quoted for the ban is graded weak (Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 131, da\'if per Darussalam). Dhikr, du\'a and listening to the Qur\'an are fine for everyone.\n- Entering the masjid: the schools differ (the Hanafi, Maliki and Hanbali schools forbid it; the Shafi\'i school allows passing through).\n\nWhen the bleeding stops, she makes ghusl and resumes prayer and fasting. The usual length and how to count it differ by school; if the bleeding is irregular or continues beyond the usual days (istihadah, bleeding of illness), the woman is treated as pure: she makes wudu for each prayer and prays and fasts as normal. Postnatal bleeding (nifas) also stops prayer and fasting and lasts until it ends, up to about forty days in the view of most scholars.\n\nFor a particular case, ask a scholar or a trusted woman scholar; this is general information only.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 335; Sahih al-Bukhari 1951; Qur\'an 2:222; Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 131, da\'if per Darussalam.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-ghusl]',
    topic: 'period',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Quran touching, entering the masjid, istihadah and the nifas limit are school positions from standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'istinja',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bistinja\b',
      r'\b(?:toilet|bathroom|lavatory|restroom|relieving myself|relieve myself|call of nature)\b.*\b(?:etiquette|manners|rules|how|clean|wash|islam|dua|sunnah|adab|qibla|hand)\b',
      r'\b(?:etiquette|manners|rules|adab|sunnah)\b.*\b(?:toilet|bathroom|lavatory|restroom|call of nature)\b',
      r'\bhow (?:to|do i) clean (?:myself )?after (?:the )?toilet\b',
      r'\bclean(?:ing)? (?:myself |yourself )?after (?:using )?(?:the )?(?:toilet|bathroom|urinat\w*|defecat\w*)\b',
      r'\btoilet paper\b',
      r'\bwater or (?:tissue|paper|toilet paper)\b',
      r'\bface (?:the )?qibla\b.*\b(?:toilet|urinat\w*|relie\w*)\b',
      r'\bwhich hand\b.*\b(?:toilet|clean|wash)\b',
      r'\bwhat (?:do|should) (?:i|we) (?:say|recite) (?:when|before) (?:entering|going to|going into) (?:the )?(?:toilet|bathroom|lavatory)\b',
      r'\burinat\w* (?:standing|while standing)\b',
      r'\b(?:pee|peeing) standing\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Cleaning yourself after the toilet is called istinja, and it is a basic part of Islamic cleanliness. The manners that come down from the Prophet ﷺ:\n- Clean yourself with water if it is available. The Prophet ﷺ\'s servant Anas used to bring a tumbler of water for this (Sahih al-Bukhari 150). Tissue, stones or similar are also enough, if used until clean and in an odd number.\n- Use the left hand for cleaning, and do not touch your private part with the right hand (Sahih al-Bukhari 153).\n- Do not face the qibla or turn your back on it in an open place when relieving yourself, and do not clean with fewer than three stones or with dung or bone (Sahih Muslim 262).\n- The du\'a on entering is "Bismillah, Allahumma inni a\'udhu bika minal-khubuthi wal-khaba\'ith" (O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the male and female devils) and on leaving "Ghufranaka". Enter with the left foot and leave with the right.\n- Keep your body and clothes free of urine splashes; guard against drops afterwards. If you pass urine standing, make sure no spray touches you.\n\nWudu is needed afterwards for prayer, since urine and stool break wudu.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 150, 153; Sahih Muslim 262.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-wudu]',
    topic: 'toilet',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The entering du\'a and leaving words and the left foot/right foot order are standard teaching, not text-checked here (the hadith cited cover the water, hand and qibla points).',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'fast-teeth',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:brush\w*|toothpaste|tooth paste|toothbrush|mouthwash|miswak|siwak|siwaak|floss\w*|clean(?:ing)? (?:my |the )?teeth|rins\w* (?:my |the )?mouth|gargl\w*|dental|dentist)\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:before prayer|every prayer|for prayer|wudu)\b(?!.*(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam))',
      r'\b(?:game|story)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Yes, you may brush your teeth while fasting. It does not break the fast as long as you do not swallow the toothpaste or water. Use a little paste, spit it all out and rinse your mouth well. If some goes down by accident, the fast is still valid: the Qur\'an says "there is no blame upon you for that in which you have erred but [only for] what your hearts intended" (Qur\'an 33:5).\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ greatly valued cleaning the teeth with the siwak: he said that if it were not hard on his followers he would order them to use it for every prayer (Sahih al-Bukhari 887). The Shafi\'i school dislikes using the siwak after midday while fasting, because of the smell of the fasting mouth, so if you follow it, brush before noon. Some scholars prefer to avoid strong-tasting paste in the day and brush at suhoor and after iftar; that is a matter of care, not a requirement.\n\nThe same is true in wudu: rinse your mouth and nose, but do not sniff water deep into your nose while fasting. The Prophet ﷺ said: snuff up water freely unless you are fasting (Sunan Abu Dawud 2366, sahih per al-Albani).\n\nSource: Qur\'an 33:5; Sahih al-Bukhari 887; Sunan Abu Dawud 2366, sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-fasting]',
    topic: 'fasting',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The Shafi\'i dislike of siwak after midday is standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here. The mouth/paste rule is the general view.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'fast-medicine',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:medicine|medication|medications|meds?|pill|pills|tablet|tablets|capsule|capsules|ibuprofen|paracetamol|acetaminophen|panadol|aspirin|antibiotic\w*|painkiller\w*|pain killer\w*|syrup|cough|insulin|diabetes|diabetic|blood pressure|prescription|drugs?)\b.*\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b',
      r'\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b.*\b(?:medicine|medication|medications|meds?|pill|pills|tablet|tablets|capsule|capsules|ibuprofen|paracetamol|acetaminophen|panadol|aspirin|antibiotic\w*|painkiller\w*|pain killer\w*|syrup|cough|insulin|diabetes|diabetic|blood pressure|prescription)\b',
      r'\b(?:take|taking|swallow\w*)\b.*\b(?:medicine|medication|pill|pills|tablet|tablets|ibuprofen|paracetamol)\b.*\b(?:while|during|when|if)\b.*\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:injection|inhaler|drops?|puffer|patch|suppository|nasal spray|spray)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Swallowed medicine such as a tablet, capsule or syrup breaks the fast, because it goes into the stomach by the mouth. This holds for ibuprofen, paracetamol, antibiotics and the like. I cannot tell you whether any medicine is right for you or safe to delay: for that, ask your doctor or pharmacist.\n\nWhat the religion gives you is ease. Allah says: "Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship" (Qur\'an 2:185). If you are ill, and fasting would worsen it or you cannot go without the medicine, you may break the fast, take the medicine, and make up the days later: "whoever among you is ill or on a journey [during them] - then an equal number of days [are to be made up]" (Qur\'an 2:184). If your illness is long-term with no hope of recovering, you give a ransom of feeding a poor person for each day instead (Qur\'an 2:184).\n\nBefore you break a fast, see if you can plan the medicine for suhoor and after iftar. Ask your doctor about long-acting forms that cover the day, and ask a scholar if your case is unclear. A person whose doctor says they must not fast should not fast; it is not weakness in faith.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:185, 2:184.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-fasting]',
    topic: 'fasting',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The fidyah for long-term illness and the general rule for oral medicine are standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'fast-injection-tests',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:injection\w*|inject\w*|vaccin\w*|jab|drip|iv|intravenous|blood test|blood tests|blood draw|donat\w* blood|give blood|cupping|insulin shot|anaesthe\w*|anesthe\w*|dialysis|catheter)\b.*\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b',
      r'\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b.*\b(?:injection\w*|vaccin\w*|jab|drip|intravenous|blood test|blood tests|blood draw|donat\w* blood|give blood|cupping|insulin shot|anaesthe\w*|anesthe\w*|dialysis|catheter|surgery)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Most contemporary scholars say these do not break the fast, as nothing is taken as food or drink by the mouth or throat: a blood test, giving blood, a vaccine, and a medical injection (such as insulin, an antibiotic or a painkiller). A drip that gives nourishment (glucose, a feeding drip) is treated like food and breaks the fast. Some scholars are stricter, so if you are unsure, plan these for after iftar.\n\nWhatever your case, if the treatment is needed, your health comes first. The Qur\'an allows the sick to break the fast and make up the days: "Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship" (Qur\'an 2:185). Ask your doctor for the timing and a scholar if your case is complicated.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:185.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-fasting]',
    topic: 'fasting',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Non-nutritional injections, blood tests and drips are the position of most contemporary fatwa bodies; not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'fast-drops-inhaler',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:eye ?drops?|ear ?drops?|nose ?drops?|nasal spray|nasal drops?|inhaler|inhalers|asthma|puffer|ventolin|nebuli[sz]er|oxygen|patch|suppository|skin cream|ointment|lotion)\b.*\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b',
      r'\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b.*\b(?:eye ?drops?|ear ?drops?|nose ?drops?|nasal spray|nasal drops?|inhaler|inhalers|asthma|puffer|ventolin|nebuli[sz]er|oxygen|patch|suppository|ointment)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'The scholars differ here, so be careful and, where you can, choose the safer course:\n- Eye drops and ear drops: most contemporary scholars say they do not break the fast, because they are not food or drink. Some classical scholars say the taste reaching the throat does. If you can use them at night, do.\n- Nose spray and drops: avoid them if you can; anything that reaches the throat is treated like drink.\n- An asthma inhaler: many contemporary fatwa bodies say it does not break the fast, since it is a vapour and not food or drink; some scholars are stricter. If asthma is a risk, the need to breathe comes first, and you can make the day up if a scholar rules it broken.\n- Skin creams, patches and ointments: these do not break the fast.\n\nIf you must use something and it may break the fast, health comes first. Allah gives ease: "Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship" (Qur\'an 2:185). Ask your doctor about timing and a scholar about your case.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:185.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-fasting]',
    topic: 'fasting',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Eye and ear drops, nose sprays, and inhalers are contested; the positions given are those of many contemporary fatwa bodies, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'fast-smoking',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:smok\w*|cigarette\w*|vap(?:e|es|ing)|vapor\w*|e ?cig\w*|shisha|hookah|nicotine|tobacco|chewing gum|gum|snuff|paan)\b.*\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b',
      r'\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b.*\b(?:smok\w*|cigarette\w*|vap(?:e|es|ing)|vapor\w*|e ?cig\w*|shisha|hookah|nicotine|tobacco|chewing gum|gum|paan)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Smoking breaks the fast: the smoke is drawn on purpose into the throat and lungs, and nicotine is taken as a substance. Vaping, shisha and chewing tobacco do too. Chewing gum is best avoided in the day: the flavour dissolves and may be swallowed. If you smoke, Ramadan is a good time to stop, with the help of your doctor: you manage without it from dawn to sunset, and many people find that is the start of quitting.\n\nIf you smoked by mistake, forgot you were fasting, or something went down without intending it, the fast is not broken: the Qur\'an says "there is no blame upon you for that in which you have erred but [only for] what your hearts intended" (Qur\'an 33:5).\n\nSource: Qur\'an 33:5.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-fasting]',
    topic: 'fasting',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Chewing gum is stated as best avoided, not as a ruling; the rest is the general view.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'fast-forgot-accident',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:forgot|forgetfully|forgotten|by mistake|accidentally|by accident|unintentionally|without (?:thinking|meaning|realizing|realising)|did not realise|didnt realise|swallowed (?:a )?(?:fly|insect|dust|smoke|water)|water (?:went|going) down|swallowed (?:my )?(?:saliva|spit)|saliva|spit|spitting|phlegm|mucus|dust|fly)\b.*\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b',
      r'\b(?:fast\w*|ramadan|roza|saum|sawm|siyam)\b.*\b(?:forgot|forgetfully|by mistake|accidentally|by accident|unintentionally|swallowed (?:a )?(?:fly|insect|dust|smoke|water)|water (?:went|going) down|saliva|phlegm|mucus|dust)\b',
      r'\b(?:ate|eat|eaten|drank|drink|drunk|had) .*\b(?:forgot|forgetting) .*\b(?:fast\w*|fasting)\b',
      r'\bforgot i was fasting\b',
      r'\bforgot (?:that )?(?:i was|it was) (?:fasting|ramadan)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'If you eat or drink because you forgot you were fasting, your fast is still valid. The Prophet ﷺ said: if somebody eats or drinks forgetfully, he should complete his fast, for what he ate or drank was given to him by Allah (Sahih al-Bukhari 1933). Stop as soon as you remember. You do not make it up.\n\nThe same ease applies to things you do not intend or cannot avoid: a fly or dust going down your throat, water going down when rinsing your mouth in wudu (provided you did not exaggerate), a dream with wetness, or your own saliva. The Qur\'an says "there is no blame upon you for that in which you have erred but [only for] what your hearts intended" (Qur\'an 33:5).\n\nPhlegm that reaches the mouth should be spat out rather than swallowed on purpose; if it goes down by itself, the fast stands.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1933; Qur\'an 33:5.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-fasting]',
    topic: 'fasting',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The treatment of phlegm and water in wudu are general views; the main ruling is cited.',
  ),
