    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:difference|vs|versus|compare)\b',
      r'\b(?:mahram|woman|women|female)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Hajj is the pilgrimage to Makkah, the fifth pillar of Islam, obligatory once in a lifetime for every Muslim who is able. Allah says: "to Allah from the people is a pilgrimage to the House - for whoever is able to find thereto a way" (Qur\'an 3:97). When the Prophet ﷺ told people it was obligatory and someone asked if it was every year, he stayed silent and then said that if he had said yes it would have been required, and it would have been too hard (Sahih Muslim 1337)\n\nHajj has fixed days in the month of Dhul Hijjah (the twelfth month):\n- 8 Dhul Hijjah (Yawm at-Tarwiyah): enter ihram (for those in Makkah) and go to Mina, praying and spending the day and night there.\n- 9 Dhul Hijjah (the Day of Arafah): stand at Arafah from after noon until sunset, in du\'a and dhikr. This is the heart of Hajj; without it there is no Hajj. After sunset, go to Muzdalifah, pray Maghrib and Isha together, sleep there and collect pebbles.\n- 10 Dhul Hijjah (Eid al-Adha): after Fajr, go to Mina and throw seven pebbles at the large pillar (Jamrat al-Aqabah), offer the sacrifice, shave or trim the hair (men), and go to Makkah for Tawaf al-Ifadah and the Sa\'i of Safa and Marwah.\n- 11, 12 (and 13 for those who stay) Dhul Hijjah: in Mina, throw seven pebbles at each of the three pillars each day after midday. Those in a hurry may leave on the 12th before sunset.\n- Farewell tawaf at the Kaaba before leaving Makkah.\n\nThe main pillars (arkan) are ihram, standing at Arafah, Tawaf al-Ifadah and the Sa\'i (the Hanafi school counts the Sa\'i as wajib instead). There are three forms: Tamattu\' (Umrah first, then Hajj), Qiran (both together) and Ifrad (Hajj alone). The Prophet ﷺ said that whoever performs Hajj for Allah\'s pleasure and avoids bad conduct returns as if newly born (Sahih al-Bukhari 1521).\n\nThe Hajj guides in this app cover each step in detail.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 3:97; Sahih Muslim 1337; Sahih al-Bukhari 1521.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-hajj-overview]\n[GO:/academy/hajj-umrah-pack]',
    topic: 'hajj',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The day-by-day order, the pillars and wajibs per school, and the forms of Hajj are standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'hajj-vs-umrah',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bhajj\b.*\bumrah\b.*\b(?:difference|different|same|vs|versus|compare|between)\b',
      r'\b(?:difference|different|between)\b.*\bhajj\b.*\bumrah\b',
      r'\bumrah\b.*\bhajj\b.*\b(?:difference|different|same|vs|versus|compare|between)\b',
      r'\bwhat is umrah\b',
      r'\bumrah (?:steps?|rites?|rituals?|explained|guide|basics|how|process)\b',
      r'\bhow (?:to|do (?:i|we|you)) (?:perform|do) umrah\b',
      r'\bsmall hajj\b',
      r'\blesser pilgrimage\b',
      r'\bumrah (?:compulsory|obligatory|fard|mandatory|required|sunnah)\b',
      r'\bis umrah (?:compulsory|obligatory|fard|mandatory|required|sunnah)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Umrah is the "lesser pilgrimage": a shorter, voluntary journey to the Kaaba that can be done at any time of the year. Hajj is the major pilgrimage with fixed days, obligatory once on every Muslim who is able.\n\nUmrah has four parts: (1) enter ihram at the miqat with the intention for Umrah, (2) circle the Kaaba seven times (tawaf), (3) walk between Safa and Marwah seven times (sa\'i), (4) shave or trim the hair (men shave or trim; women trim a fingertip\'s length). That is all; it takes a few hours.\n\nHajj includes similar acts (ihram, tawaf, sa\'i) but adds standing at Arafah, the night at Muzdalifah, stoning the pillars in Mina, and the sacrifice, over five or six fixed days in Dhul Hijjah.\n\nThe difference in ruling: Hajj is a pillar of Islam. Umrah is obligatory once for those able in the Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools, and a strongly recommended sunnah in the Hanafi and Maliki schools. The Prophet ﷺ said that one Umrah is an expiation for the sins between it and the previous one, and the reward of an accepted Hajj is nothing less than Paradise (Sahih al-Bukhari 1773). Umrah does not replace Hajj.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1773.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-umrah]\n[GO:/academy/hajj-umrah-pack]',
    topic: 'umrah',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The ruling on Umrah per school and the details of trimming are standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'credit-card',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bcredit cards?\b',
      r'\bcharge cards?\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:debt|nisab|zakat)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'A credit card is a loan tool, so the answer depends on how it is used, not on the plastic. The problem is interest (riba): "Allah has permitted trade and has forbidden interest" (Qur\'an 2:275), and the Prophet ﷺ cursed everyone involved in it (Sahih Muslim 1598).\n\n- Carrying a balance that is charged interest is riba, and it is not allowed.\n- Paying the full balance before any interest is charged: many contemporary scholars allow having and using the card on that basis, as long as you are sure you can pay in time and you do not take on interest or late charges you could avoid.\n- Some scholars are stricter and say not to take a conventional card at all, because the agreement you sign states interest on late payment.\n- Safer options: a debit card, a charge card that must be paid in full each month, or a card from an Islamic provider where one exists.\n\nWhich view to follow in your own case is for a qualified scholar. The Tayyib Finance section of this app lists halal options.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:275; Sahih Muslim 1598.\n[GO:/finance]',
    topic: 'credit cards',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The two scholarly positions (allowed when paid in full; avoid the conventional card) are standard contemporary teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'riba-interest',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\briba\b',
      r'\binterest\b.*\b(?:haram|halal|islam|allowed|permissible|forbidden|prohibited|sin|bank|loan|mortgage|savings|account|credit)\b',
      r'\b(?:haram|halal|islam|allowed|permissible|forbidden|prohibited|sin)\b.*\binterest\b',
      r'\busury\b',
      r'\bbank interest\b',
      r'\binterest (?:on|from|in) (?:my )?(?:savings|bank|account|loan|deposit)\b',
      r'\bwhat to do with (?:bank )?interest\b',
      r'\bsavings account\b',
      r'\bcredit cards?\b',
      r'\bpersonal loans?\b',
      r'\bstudent loans?\b',
      r'\bcar loans?\b',
      r'\bis (?:it )?(?:haram|halal|ok|okay|allowed) to (?:take|get|have|use) (?:a )?(?:loan|credit)\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:mortgage|house loan|home loan)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Riba (interest, usury) is forbidden in Islam. Allah says: "Allah has permitted trade and has forbidden interest" (Qur\'an 2:275), and "fear Allah and give up what remains [due to you] of interest, if you should be believers" (Qur\'an 2:278-279). The Prophet ﷺ cursed the one who takes interest, the one who pays it, the one who records it, and the two witnesses (Sahih Muslim 1598).\n\nWhat this covers: interest on loans and credit cards, interest on savings accounts and fixed deposits, and any agreement where a loan carries an extra amount stated in advance. Paying a late fee that grows with time is interest too.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Borrow only when needed, from interest-free sources first (family, a qard hasan loan from a community fund, an employer).\n- Use a halal alternative: Islamic banks and finance firms (profit-sharing, lease-to-own, cost-plus sale), where available.\n- A credit card that you pay off in full every month, so no interest is ever charged, is considered acceptable by many scholars, though some avoid cards with interest-bearing terms; ask a scholar.\n- Interest your bank puts into your account: take it out and give it away to the poor or for public good, with no intention of reward for yourself (the view of most scholars today). Do not keep it. If you can, move to an interest-free account.\n- If you are already in a loan with interest, you do not tear up the contract, but you repent, pay it off as soon as you can, and avoid new ones.\n\nFor mortgages, see the separate answer. Ask a scholar for your own situation. The Tayyib Finance section of this app lists halal options.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:275, 2:278-279; Sahih Muslim 1598.\n[GO:/finance]',
