  CoreEntry(
    id: 'seafood-shrimp',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:shrimp|prawn|prawns|crab|lobster|oyster|oysters|clam|clams|mussel|mussels|squid|calamari|octopus|shellfish|seafood|sea food|eel|catfish|crustacean\w*|scallop\w*|fish)\b.*\b(?:halal|haram|allowed|permissible|permitted|eat|forbidden|makruh|islam)\b',
      r'\b(?:halal|haram|allowed|permissible|permitted|forbidden|makruh)\b.*\b(?:shrimp|prawn|prawns|crab|lobster|oyster|oysters|clam|clams|mussel|mussels|squid|calamari|octopus|shellfish|seafood|sea food|eel|catfish|crustacean\w*|scallop\w*|fish)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Most of the seafood you ask about is halal. The Qur\'an says "Lawful to you is game from the sea and its food as provision for you and the travelers" (Qur\'an 5:96). When a man asked the Prophet ﷺ about wudu with sea water, he said its water is pure and what dies in it is lawful to eat (Sunan Abu Dawud 83, sahih per al-Albani).\n\nThe schools differ on the detail:\n- The Maliki, Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools: all sea animals are halal (with some exceptions for harmful ones in some views). That includes fish, shrimp, prawns, crab, lobster, squid and octopus.\n- The Hanafi school: fish only is halal without question. Shrimp and prawns are considered fish by many Hanafi scholars and are permitted; other shellfish such as crab and lobster are disliked or forbidden for strict Hanafis, and many Hanafi scholars today allow them.\n\nSo shrimp is halal in all the schools according to most scholars; if you follow the Hanafi school strictly, ask your scholar about crab, lobster and other shellfish. Seafood cooked in alcohol or fried in oil also used for pork is another matter.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 5:96; Sunan Abu Dawud 83, sahih per al-Albani.',
    topic: 'seafood',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Hanafi view on shellfish other than fish and shrimp is described in general terms and varies among Hanafi scholars; not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'gelatin',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bgelatin\w*\b',
      r'\bgelatine\b',
      r'\bmarshmallows?\b',
      r'\bgummy\b',
      r'\bgummies\b',
      r'\bjello\b',
      r'\bjelly\b.*\b(?:halal|haram)\b',
      r'\bcapsules?\b.*\b(?:gelatin|halal|haram)\b',
      r'\bvaccin\w*\b.*\b(?:pork|gelatin|haram|halal)\b',
      r'\bhidden (?:haram|ingredients?)\b',
      r'\bfood (?:additives?|e numbers?|ingredients?)\b',
      r'\be ?[0-9]{3}\b.*\b(?:halal|haram)\b',
      r'\bhow (?:do|to) (?:i )?check (?:if )?(?:food|a product|ingredients?) (?:is )?halal\b',
      r'\bis this halal\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Gelatin is made from animal skin and bones, usually pork or beef. Pork is forbidden (Qur\'an 2:173), so:\n- Pork gelatin is haram.\n- Gelatin from beef or fish is halal if the cattle were slaughtered in the Islamic way (for beef) or it is from fish.\n- Gelatin when the source is not stated: scholars differ. Some (including several fiqh councils) hold that the bones and skin undergo a complete chemical change (istihalah) and so the final product is pure and lawful; others say pork remains pork, so it is not allowed. Because of this real difference, the careful course is to avoid gelatin of unknown source and to choose products marked halal, fish gelatin, pectin or agar.\n\nHow to check: look for a halal logo from a certifier you trust; "gelatin" alone does not tell you the animal. Other hidden items include alcohol in flavourings, pork-derived enzymes, lard and some emulsifiers (E441 is gelatin; E120, carmine, is from insects and disputed). Marshmallows, gummy sweets, jelly desserts and some capsules and vaccines commonly contain gelatin.\n\nFor medicines and vaccines, many scholars allow them even if gelatin of uncertain source is in them, when there is a need and no alternative; ask your doctor and a scholar.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:173.',
    topic: 'gelatin',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The istihalah debate on gelatin, carmine, and the medicine allowance are contested positions; flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'halal-meat',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bhalal meat\b',
      r'\bzabiha\b',
      r'\bzabihah\b',
      r'\bdhabiha\b',
      r'\bslaughter\w*\b',
