    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:najis|najasa|impure)\b',
      r'\b(?:game|story|stories)\b',
      r'\bcats?\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Dogs: scholars differ on keeping and touching them.\n- Keeping a dog as a house pet is discouraged: the Prophet ﷺ said that whoever keeps a dog that is neither a watch dog nor a hunting dog loses two qirat of his good deeds every day (Sahih al-Bukhari 5480). Keeping a dog for hunting, herding or guarding is allowed, and most scholars add a guide or service dog for a person who needs it.\n- Touching a dog or its saliva: the Shafi\'i and Hanbali schools treat the saliva as impure and require washing seven times; the Maliki school treats the dog as pure; see the impurity answer. Dry contact is not a problem in any school.\n- The angels: the Prophet ﷺ said the angels of mercy do not enter a house where there is a dog or a picture of a living creature (Sahih al-Bukhari 3225). Scholars explain this in different ways.\n\nWhatever you decide, be kind to dogs: cruelty to any animal is a sin, and feeding or giving water to a thirsty one is rewarded.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 5480, 3225.',
    topic: 'pets',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Dogs: the angels hadith is explained variously by scholars; a guide dog for the blind is a standard fiqh allowance; not text-checked here.',
  ),
