      r'\bhair dye\b',
      r'\bhair colou?r\w*\b',
      r'\bcolou?r(?:ing)? (?:my |the )?hair\b',
      r'\bblack dye\b',
      r'\bgrey hair\b',
      r'\bgray hair\b',
      r'\bwhite hair\b',
      r'\bhair extensions?\b',
      r'\bwigs?\b',
      r'\bbleach(?:ing)? (?:my |the )?hair\b',
      r'\bhighlights\b.*\bhair\b',
      r'\bcut(?:ting)? (?:my |the )?hair\b',
      r'\bhaircut\b',
      r'\bqaza\b',
      r'\bshav\w* (?:my )?(?:head|legs?|armpits?|pubic)\b',
      r'\bfitrah\b.*\b(?:nails?|hair)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Hair colour:\n- Grey or white hair: the Prophet ﷺ told Abu Bakr\'s father, whose hair and beard were white, to change the colour with something but to avoid black (Sahih Muslim 2102). So henna, or a brown or yellow dye, is recommended; black is disliked by most scholars (some scholars allow it for women, or in war).\n- Dyeing hair that is not grey for fashion, in a non-black colour, is allowed for both men and women by most scholars, as long as the dye is not a barrier that stops water from reaching the hair (it must be washable-through in wudu: stains are fine; paint-like coatings are not).\n- Bleaching or highlights: allowed, within modesty, as long as you are not imitating a way of life that Islam forbids.\n\nHair extensions: attaching other people\'s hair is forbidden in the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 5937; most scholars apply it to real human hair, and some extend it to anything attached that looks like your own. Wigs: scholars differ.\n\nCutting hair: allowed for men and women, with modesty. A woman may trim her hair; she should not cut it to imitate men.\n\nBody hair: removing pubic and underarm hair, clipping the nails and trimming the moustache are part of the fitrah, the natural practices (Sahih Muslim 258). Removing hair elsewhere, such as legs or arms, is not forbidden for a woman; men should not imitate women in ways that are specific to them. Shaving the whole head is allowed for a man, and is part of the rites of Hajj and Umrah.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 2102, 258; Sahih al-Bukhari 5937.',
    topic: 'hair',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Dye colours, extensions and wigs are contested; the positions are standard fiqh teaching, not text-checked here.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'birthday-festivals',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bbirthdays?\b',
      r'\bcelebrat\w*\b.*\b(?:christmas|easter|halloween|diwali|new year|new years|valentine\w*|thanksgiving|mothers day|fathers day|anniversary|holi|lunar new year|chinese new year|hanukkah|st patrick\w*|april fool\w*|mawlid|milad|prophets birthday|shab e barat|nisf shaban)\b',
      r'\b(?:christmas|easter|halloween|diwali|new year|new years|valentine\w*|thanksgiving|mothers day|fathers day|holi|lunar new year|chinese new year|hanukkah|mawlid|milad|prophets birthday|shab e barat|nisf shaban)\b.*\b(?:muslims?|islam|haram|halal|allowed|permissible|celebrate|greet|wish)\b',
      r'\bmerry christmas\b',
      r'\bhappy (?:new year|diwali|christmas|easter|birthday|holidays?)\b',
      r'\bwish(?:ing)? (?:my |a )?(?:non muslim|christian|friend|neighbou?r|colleague)\w*\b.*\b(?:christmas|easter|diwali|holiday)\b',
      r'\bcan muslims? celebrate\b',
      r'\bis it (?:haram|allowed|ok|okay) to celebrate\b',
      r'\bgift\w* (?:to|for) (?:a )?non muslim\b',
      r'\bnon muslim (?:holiday|festival|celebration)\w*\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Islam has two festivals for the community: Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha. The Prophet ﷺ said there is an Eid for every people and this is ours (Sahih al-Bukhari 952).\n\nBirthdays: scholars differ. Many scholars say that marking a birthday as a regular celebration is not from the practice of the Prophet ﷺ and his companions, and so avoid it, and some of them consider it an imitation of others. Other scholars see a birthday party or a gift as a social custom, not worship, and so permitted, as long as it has nothing forbidden in it and is not treated as a religious ritual. Being kind to people on their day, thanking Allah for your life, giving thanks and making du\'a is good on any day. Whichever view you follow, do not make it a matter for quarrelling in the family.\n\nFestivals of other faiths (Christmas, Diwali, Easter and so on): most scholars say a Muslim should not take part in the religious rituals or the religious side of another faith\'s festival, nor give gifts that mark it as worship. Allah also asks us to be good and just to neighbours and co-workers of other faiths: "Allah does not forbid you from those who do not fight you because of religion and do not expel you from your homes - from being righteous toward them and acting justly toward them" (Qur\'an 60:8). Many scholars therefore allow a polite greeting or a card in return for theirs, and attending a family or workplace event without joining in the ritual; others are stricter and prefer not to congratulate on a religious day. Both are held by scholars.\n\nMawlid (the Prophet\'s birthday), Shab-e-Barat and similar nights: scholars differ widely, some treating them as good remembrance, others as additions to the religion. I will not rule between them; follow a scholar you trust and avoid anything forbidden.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 952; Qur\'an 60:8.',
    topic: 'celebrations',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Birthdays, congratulating non-Muslims on festivals, and Mawlid are contested; described as differences among scholars; flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'voting-politics',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bvot(?:e|ing|ed)\b',
      r'\belections?\b',
      r'\bpolitics\b',
      r'\bpolitical part\w*\b',
      r'\bdemocracy\b',
      r'\bdemocratic\b',
      r'\bparticipat\w* in (?:politics|government|elections?)\b',
      r'\bwork(?:ing)? (?:for|in) (?:the )?(?:government|police|army|military)\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:vote for (?:me|my)|game|poll in (?:the )?app)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Scholars differ on voting and political participation in non-Muslim-majority countries and under man-made systems:\n- Many contemporary scholars, and fiqh councils for Muslim minorities, say voting is allowed and even encouraged where it helps the community, reduces harm, protects Muslims\' rights, or brings a better candidate. They argue from the principle of weighing benefit and harm, and from the Prophet\'s own treaties and just dealings.\n- Some scholars say it is not allowed, because it involves taking part in a system that is not built on Islamic law, or because the candidates support what is forbidden.\n- Between these, some say it depends on the circumstances: vote for the lesser harm, or for a candidate who defends justice, when not voting would bring greater harm.\n\nAllah commands justice and honesty in trusts and in rule; Muslims in any country are told to be good citizens who obey the law as long as it does not command disobedience to Allah, and to be just to all. This app takes no side in politics and does not tell you whom to vote for. Ask a local scholar who knows the situation where you live.',
    topic: 'voting',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Scholars differ on voting; described neutrally; flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'music',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bmusic\b',
      r'\bmusical instruments?\b',
      r'\bsinging\b',
      r'\bsongs?\b',
      r'\bnasheeds?\b',
      r'\banasheed\b',
      r'\bduff\b',
      r'\bdrums?\b',
      r'\bguitar\b',
      r'\bpiano\b',
      r'\bconcerts?\b',
      r'\bflute\b',
      r'\bdancing\b',
      r'\blistening to (?:music|songs?|the radio)\b',
