    answer: 'Scholars have different views here, and it depends on the game.\n\n- Gambling: any game played for money or a stake is gambling and forbidden (Qur\'an 5:90, and see the answer on gambling). That includes card games with stakes, and loot boxes or paid chances in video games that work like a lottery.\n- Backgammon and dice games: the Prophet ﷺ condemned backgammon in strong words (Sahih Muslim 2260). Many classical scholars apply this to games decided by dice, such as Ludo; others say the report is about backgammon and gambling and does not cover every dice game. Scholars differ on this.\n- Chess: the schools differed. The Shafi\'i school held it to be disliked rather than forbidden; the Hanafi, Maliki and Hanbali schools held it to be forbidden or strongly disliked, especially when it takes the place of prayer or is played for stakes. Many scholars today allow it as a mental exercise when there is no betting, it does not make you miss prayers or your duties, and it is not played to excess. Please follow the scholar you trust.\n- Video games and other games: the game itself is generally allowed when its content is lawful (no immodesty, no gambling, no mockery of religion), and when it does not take you from prayer, family and work. Allah says that Satan wants to turn people from the remembrance of Allah and from prayer through intoxicants and gambling (Qur\'an 5:91), so watch the time you spend.\n\nIn this app, the games are made to teach and have no betting, coins, ratings or purchases.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 5:90, 5:91; Sahih Muslim 2260.\n[GO:/games]',
