      r'\b(?:husband|wife) (?:beats|hits|abuses) me\b',
      r'\bdomestic (?:violence|abuse)\b.*\b(?:me|my|i)\b',
      r'\bi (?:am|m) a victim of (?:domestic|abuse)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'I am sorry. What is happening to you is not your fault, and Islam does not ask you to bear violence.\n\nFirst, your safety: if you are in danger right now, go to a safe place and call your local emergency number. Tell someone you trust (a relative, a friend, a doctor), and keep a record of injuries and messages. A local domestic-violence service, which you can find through your doctor or an online search for your country, can help in secret.\n\nIslam gives you rights: Allah commands living with a spouse "in kindness" (Qur\'an 4:19), the Prophet ﷺ never struck a woman or a servant with his hand (Sahih Muslim 2328), and when a husband broke his wife\'s arm, the Prophet ﷺ ordered that she be set free from him (Sunan an-Nasa\'i 3497, sahih per al-Albani). A wife can ask for separation by khul\' or through a court, and a girl or woman does not need to stay with someone who harms her.\n\nAn imam or scholar can support you, but you do not have to ask permission to protect yourself, and you should not be asked to keep quiet. If you want, you can also ask me about khul\' and divorce in general.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 4:19; Sahih Muslim 2328; Sunan an-Nasa\'i 3497, sahih per al-Albani.',
    topic: 'abuse',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Khul and court separation are described in general terms; nothing here is a legal ruling.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
