      r'\b(?:wife|wives|women|woman)\b.*\b(?:hit|hits|hitting|beat|beaten|beating|slap|strike|striking|struck)\b',
      r'\bhusband\b.*\b(?:allowed|permitted|right|permission)\b.*\b(?:hit|beat|strike|discipline)\b',
      r'\b4 ?:? ?34\b',
      r'\bnushuz\b',
      r'\bwife beating\b',
      r'\bbeat(?:ing)? (?:their|your|his|a) wi(?:fe|ves)\b',
      r'\bdomestic (?:violence|abuse)\b',
      r'\bmen are in charge of women\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:my (?:husband|father|dad|brother|in laws?) (?:hits|beats))\b',
    ],
    answer: 'No. Hitting a wife in anger, to control her or to make her afraid, is wrong in Islam, and a man who does it is answerable to Allah for it. The Prophet ﷺ never did it. A\'isha reported that the Prophet never beat anyone with his hand, neither a woman nor a servant, except when fighting in the cause of Allah (Sahih Muslim 2328). He also said: None of you should flog his wife as he flogs a slave and then have sexual intercourse with her in the last part of the day (Sahih al-Bukhari 5204).\n\nThe verse people quote is "But those [wives] from whom you fear arrogance - [first] advise them; [then if they persist], forsake them in bed; and [finally], strike them. But if they obey you [once more], seek no means against them" (Qur\'an 4:34). It is about a husband facing serious, persistent misconduct, it comes last after advice and separation in bed, and the scholars restrict it heavily:\n- In his farewell sermon the Prophet ﷺ allowed only a beating that does not harm (Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 1163, hasan per al-Albani).\n- Never on the face, and never insulting her (Sunan Abu Dawud 2142, hasan sahih per al-Albani).\n- Many scholars say that at most it is symbolic, such as a touch with a folded cloth or a siwak, that causes no pain, bruise or mark and no humiliation, and that leaving it is better. In today\'s world it should not be used, and any hurt or injury is a sin and a crime.\n- When many women went to his family to complain about their husbands, he said those men are not the best of you (Sunan Ibn Majah 1985, sahih per Darussalam).\n\nThe right course in a marital problem is to speak kindly, make peace, and, if needed, bring two fair relatives (Qur\'an 4:35). Allah describes marriage as "He placed between you affection and mercy" (Qur\'an 30:21), and Abu Hurairah narrated the Prophet said the most complete of the believers in faith is the one with the best character, and the best of you are those who are best to your women (Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 1162, hasan sahih per al-Albani).\n\nIf you are being hit, or you are afraid for your safety, ask me about abuse and I will tell you what you can do.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 2328; Sahih al-Bukhari 5204; Qur\'an 4:34, 4:35, 30:21; Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 1163, hasan per al-Albani; Sunan Abu Dawud 2142, hasan sahih per al-Albani; Sunan Ibn Majah 1985, sahih per Darussalam; Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 1162, hasan sahih per al-Albani.',
    topic: 'wife',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The restriction of 4:34 to a symbolic act (siwak/folded cloth) is reported in classical tafsir attributed to some companions; the attribution was not text-checked here and is stated without a named source. Flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'nikah-basics',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bnikah\b',
