    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:games?|story|prophets marriage|marriages of the prophet)\b',
      r'\b(?:non muslim|christian|jewish|jew|hindu|buddhist|atheist)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Marriage (nikah) is a sunnah the Prophet ﷺ encouraged, and he forbade giving up marriage for worship (Sunan Ibn Majah 1849, sahih per Darussalam). It is a strong covenant: Allah calls it a solemn covenant (Qur\'an 4:21), and "He placed between you affection and mercy" (Qur\'an 30:21).\n\nWhat makes a nikah valid (the schools differ in detail):\n- Consent of both. A woman must not be married against her will: The Prophet said a matron should not be given in marriage without consulting her, and a virgin not without her permission, which is her silence (Sahih al-Bukhari 5136). A forced marriage is wrong.\n- A guardian (wali) for the woman: Abu Musa narrated the Messenger of Allah said there is no marriage except with a wali (Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 1101, sahih per Darussalam). The Hanafi school allows a mature woman to contract her own marriage with the same conditions of suitability; the other schools require the wali.\n- Mahr (bridal gift) given to the wife, which belongs to her: "And give the women [upon marriage] their [bridal] gifts graciously" (Qur\'an 4:4). Its amount is by agreement and does not have to be large.\n- Two witnesses and an open announcement (as most scholars require), and an offer and acceptance in clear words. A feast (walima) follows.\n\nSeek a person of faith and character; the Prophet ﷺ said that a woman is married for wealth, family, beauty and religion, so choose the one with religion (Sahih al-Bukhari 5090).\n\nA couple living together without a valid nikah is not married, whatever the civil paper says, and a nikah without registration may be a problem for your rights in your country, so many scholars advise doing both.\n\nSource: Sunan Ibn Majah 1849, sahih per Darussalam; Qur\'an 4:21, 30:21, 4:4; Sahih al-Bukhari 5136, 5090; Jami\' at-Tirmidhi 1101, sahih per Darussalam.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-nikah]\n[GO:/academy/family-life]',
    topic: 'marriage',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The detail of conditions (witnesses, wali, Hanafi view on a woman contracting herself) varies by school; described in general terms. Flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'divorce-overview',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bdivorce\b',
      r'\btalaq\b',
      r'\btalaaq\b',
      r'\bkhula\w*\b',
      r'\biddah\b',
      r'\biddat\b',
      r'\bwaiting period\b.*\b(?:divorce|widow)\b',
      r'\bseparat\w*\b.*\b(?:husband|wife|marriage)\b',
      r'\bwhat is islams view on divorce\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Divorce is allowed in Islam as a last resort, but it is the most disliked of the permitted things in the view of Allah: this is reported in a hadith, which some scholars grade weak, so I do not rely on it. What is firmly established is the Qur\'an\'s guidance: "Divorce is twice. Then, either keep [her] in an acceptable manner or release [her] with good treatment" (Qur\'an 2:229), and "retain them according to acceptable terms or part with them according to acceptable terms" (Qur\'an 65:2).\n\nHow it works, in outline:\n- Before divorce, try to settle: speak, bring a fair relative from each side (Qur\'an 4:35), or make a settlement (Qur\'an 4:128).\n- Talaq is pronounced by the husband. The wife waits a set period (iddah) so that they may reconcile, and he must not turn her out of the house during it (Qur\'an 65:1).\n- The wife can also seek separation: khul\' (she returns the dower and is released), as the Prophet ﷺ did in the case of Thabit ibn Qais\'s wife (Sahih al-Bukhari 5273), or through a court.\n- A wife should not ask for divorce without a good reason: Thawban reported the Prophet said if any woman asks her husband for divorce without a strong reason, the odour of Paradise will be forbidden to her (Sunan Abu Dawud 2226, sahih per al-Albani).\n- Children and money matters: the rights of the wife and children stay, including maintenance and kind treatment.\n\nThe details are where the schools and the laws of each country differ, in particular on repeated or written talaq, so I cannot say whether a divorce has taken place in your case. Please ask a qualified scholar or Islamic court with the exact words and circumstances.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:229, 65:2, 4:35, 4:128, 65:1; Sahih al-Bukhari 5273; Sunan Abu Dawud 2226, sahih per al-Albani.',
    topic: 'divorce',
    reviewNote: 'Hadith on divorce being most hated is mentioned as disputed in grade and not relied on. The outline of talaq/khul/iddah is general; case details to scholars.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'interfaith-marriage',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:marry|marrying|marriage|married|wed|date|dating)\b.*\b(?:non muslim|christian|jewish|jew|hindu|buddhist|sikh|atheist|kafir|disbeliever|people of the book|ahl al kitab)\b',
      r'\b(?:non muslim|christian|jewish|jew|hindu|buddhist|sikh|atheist|kafir|disbeliever)\b.*\b(?:marry|marrying|marriage|husband|wife|spouse)\b',
      r'\binterfaith\b',
      r'\binter faith\b',
      r'\bmixed marriage\b',
      r'\bmarry outside (?:islam|the faith)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'The rulings differ for a man and a woman:\n- A Muslim woman may not marry a non-Muslim man. This is agreed among scholars, based on Qur\'an 2:221 and "they are not lawful [wives] for them, nor are they lawful [husbands] for them" (Qur\'an 60:10). He may marry her if he accepts Islam.\n- A Muslim man may marry a chaste woman from the People of the Book (Jews and Christians): "And [lawful in marriage are] chaste women from among the believers and chaste women from among those who were given the Scripture before you, when you have given them their due compensation" (Qur\'an 5:5). Most scholars allow it, with the warning that the children should be raised on Islam and that it is preferable to marry a Muslim. Some modern scholars discourage it where it harms the family\'s faith. Marriage with a woman who follows no scripture (an atheist, or most other religions) is not allowed until she becomes Muslim; scholars differ on some groups.\n\nAllah also said: "And do not marry polytheistic women until they believe" (Qur\'an 2:221). Practical advice: raise this early, and ask a scholar who knows your law and circumstances.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:221, 60:10, 5:5.',
    topic: 'interfaith',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Interfaith marriage rules: the general majority position is described; groups outside the People of the Book vary. Flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
