  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'polygamy',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bpolygam\w*\b',
      r'\bpolygyn\w*\b',
      r'\b(?:four|4|two|second|more than one) wi(?:fe|ves)\b',
      r'\bmarry (?:two|three|four|more than one)\b',
      r'\bwhy can (?:a )?(?:muslim )?(?:man|men) (?:have|marry) (?:more|four|multiple)\b',
      r'\bsecond marriage\b',
      r'\bsecond wife\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Islam permits a man to marry up to four wives, with a strict condition of justice. The Qur\'an says: "marry those that please you of [other] women, two or three or four. But if you fear that you will not be just, then [marry only] one" (Qur\'an 4:3). And it also says that perfect equality in feeling is beyond people (Qur\'an 4:129), so what is required is fairness in what he can control: housing, maintenance, time and treatment. Not being able to be just means he should marry only one.\n\nIt is a permission, not a duty or a recommendation for everyone, and many Muslims never practise it. Cruelty or neglect of a wife is a sin, and a woman can set a condition in her marriage contract that her husband will not take a second wife, which many scholars accept. The law of the country you live in also applies, and in many countries a second marriage needs to be registered or is not allowed.\n\nScholars differ on whether the first wife\'s consent is needed; a husband should, at minimum, be honest with her and fair.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 4:3, 4:129.',
    topic: 'polygamy',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Whether the first wife\'s consent is required is described as a difference; the stipulation that a contract may bar a second marriage is the view of some schools, not text-checked.',
  ),
