    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:app|screen|kids mode|kids section|kids zone|child mode|game|games)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Children are a trust. Allah says: "O you who have believed, protect yourselves and your families from a Fire" (Qur\'an 66:6), and the Prophet ﷺ showed mercy to children, saying Whoever is not merciful to others will not be treated mercifully (Sahih al-Bukhari 5997).\n\nWorship: parents are told to teach it gradually. The Prophet said command a boy to pray when he reaches the age of seven years, and when he becomes ten, beat him for prayer (Sunan Abu Dawud 494, hasan sahih per al-Albani). Scholars explain the beating as only a light, non-injurious reminder, if at all, and most advise gentleness, patience and example instead. Fasting and hijab are not obligatory until puberty; families begin training earlier in a gentle way, with encouragement and no pressure that harms the child.\n\nFairness: do not favour one child over another in gifts. The Prophet ﷺ asked a man whether he had given the like of the gift to each of his sons (Sahih al-Bukhari 2587). Never kill or harm children from fear of poverty: "do not kill your children for fear of poverty. We provide for them and for you" (Qur\'an 17:31).\n\nHitting a child as a punishment in anger, or in a way that hurts or leaves a mark, is not from the guidance of the Prophet ﷺ. Teach by love, example, du\'a and patience. The For Children section of this app is made for children to learn in a safe way.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 66:6, 17:31; Sahih al-Bukhari 5997, 2587; Sunan Abu Dawud 494, hasan sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-children-deen]',
    topic: 'children',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Scholars on discipline of children: described as gentle and non-injurious; the exact allowances vary. Flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'pillars-iman',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bpillars? of (?:iman|imaan|faith|belief)\b',
      r'\b(?:six|6) (?:pillars|articles) of (?:iman|imaan|faith|belief)\b',
      r'\barticles of (?:faith|belief|iman)\b',
      r'\bwhat (?:do|does) (?:muslims?|islam) believe\b',
      r'\bwhat is iman\b',
      r'\bwhat is (?:imaan|faith)$',
      r'\bbasic beliefs? (?:of|in) islam\b',
      r'\bbeliefs? of (?:muslims?|islam)\b',
      r'\bwhat must (?:a )?muslim believe\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Iman (faith) has six articles. The angel Jibreel once asked the Prophet ﷺ about iman. The Prophet said that iman is to affirm your faith in Allah, in His angels, in His Books, in His Messengers, in the Day of Judgment, and to affirm your faith in the divine decree about good and evil (Sahih Muslim 8).\n\n1. Allah: that He is One, with no partner (Qur\'an 112:1).\n2. His angels.\n3. His books: the Qur\'an, and the earlier revelations as they were sent down (such as the Torah and the Gospel).\n4. His messengers: from Adam to Muhammad ﷺ, the last of them (Qur\'an 33:40).\n5. The Last Day: the resurrection, reckoning, Paradise and the Fire.\n6. The divine decree (qadar): that everything happens by Allah\'s knowledge and will, while we are still responsible for our choices.\n\nThe Qur\'an lists the first five in Qur\'an 2:285 and Qur\'an 4:136. The five pillars of Islam are separate: the shahada, prayer, zakat, fasting in Ramadan and Hajj (Sahih al-Bukhari 8).\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 8; Qur\'an 112:1, 33:40, 2:285, 4:136; Sahih al-Bukhari 8.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-what-is-islam]\n[GO:/new-muslim]',
    topic: 'iman',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'None beyond the cited texts; the one-line gloss of qadar is a standard summary.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'become-muslim',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:become|becoming|convert|converting|revert|reverting|embrace|embracing|accept|accepting|join) (?:to )?(?:a )?(?:islam|muslim)\b',
      r'\bi want to (?:become|be|convert|revert|embrace|accept|join)\b.*\b(?:muslim|islam)\b',
      r'\bhow (?:do|can|to) (?:i|someone|one|you) (?:become|convert|revert|embrace|accept|join)\b',
      r'\bshahada\b',
      r'\bshahadah\b',
      r'\btestimony of faith\b',
      r'\bconversion to islam\b',
      r'\bhow to (?:become|convert|revert) \w*\b',
      r'\bnew muslim\b',
      r'\bjust (?:converted|reverted|became a muslim)\b',
      r'\bi (?:recently )?(?:converted|reverted)\b',
      r'\bwhat do i need to do to (?:become|be) (?:a )?muslim\b',
      r'\bwelcome to islam\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Welcome. Becoming a Muslim is simple: say the shahada (the declaration of faith), knowing what it means and meaning it:\n\nأشهد أن لا إله إلا الله وأشهد أن محمداً رسول الله\nAsh-hadu an la ilaha illa Allah, wa ash-hadu anna Muhammadan rasulu Allah.\n"I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."\n\nYou can say it alone, to Allah, at any time; no priest, certificate or special place is needed. Many people say it before a witness or at a mosque so that the community knows and can help, and that is a good thing to do, but it is not what makes you a Muslim. This is the first of the five pillars (Sahih al-Bukhari 8).\n\nGood to know:\n- Your earlier sins are forgiven. Amr ibn al-As reported the Prophet asked him, Are you not aware that Islam wipes out all the previous misdeeds? (Sahih Muslim 121).\n- Taking a bath on accepting Islam is recommended: Qays ibn Asim came to the Prophet intending to embrace Islam, and the Prophet commanded him to take a bath with water boiled with the leaves of the lote tree (Sunan Abu Dawud 355, sahih per Darussalam).\n- Learn step by step: first to pray (see the How to Pray guide), then fasting, and the rest as you can. Nobody expects you to know everything at once. Allah says "has not placed upon you in the religion any difficulty" (Qur\'an 22:78).\n\nThe New Muslim path in this app walks you through the basics. If you want to talk with someone, a nearby masjid will welcome you.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 8; Sahih Muslim 121; Sunan Abu Dawud 355, sahih per Darussalam; Qur\'an 22:78.\n[GO:/new-muslim]\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-prayer]',
    topic: 'shahada',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The bath on accepting Islam and what to say are standard practice; Arabic shahada is the universally known text.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'bismillah-meaning',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bbismillah\b',
      r'\bbismillahi\b',
      r'\bbasmala\w*\b',
      r'\bbasmalah\b',
      r'\bin the name of allah\b.*\bmean\w*\b',
      r'\bwhy (?:do )?muslims? say bismillah\b',
      r'\bwhen (?:to|should i) say bismillah\b',
    ],
    avoid: <String>[
      r'\b(?:slaughter\w*|halal meat|zabiha)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'Bismillah is short for "Bismillahi r-Rahmani r-Rahim": "In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful" (Qur\'an 1:1). It is the first verse of the Qur\'an\'s opening chapter, and it begins every surah except Surah At-Tawbah.\n\nMuslims say it to begin an action in Allah\'s name: eating, drinking, entering the home, starting wudu, studying, travelling. Teach children to begin with Allah\'s name. Umar ibn Abi Salama said the Prophet told him to mention the name of Allah, eat with his right hand and eat from what is nearest to him (Sahih al-Bukhari 5376). Saying "Bismillah" reminds you that you do it with Allah\'s help and for what He allows.\n\nAt the end, "Alhamdulillah" (all praise is due to Allah) is said to thank Him.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 1:1; Sahih al-Bukhari 5376.\n[GO:/adhkar]',
    topic: 'bismillah',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Only cited texts.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'salam-meaning',
    patterns: <String>[
