    answer: '"As-salamu alaykum" means "peace be upon you". The reply is "Wa alaykumu s-salam" ("and upon you peace"), and Muslims often add "wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuh" ("and Allah\'s mercy and blessings"). It is the greeting of Muslims, and it is also a prayer for the other person.\n\nThe Qur\'an commands: "And when you are greeted with a greeting, greet [in return] with one better than it or [at least] return it [in a like manner]" (Qur\'an 4:86). The Prophet ﷺ was asked what is best in Islam and said to feed people and to greet those you know and those you do not know (Sahih al-Bukhari 12).\n\nSource: Qur\'an 4:86; Sahih al-Bukhari 12.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-greeting]',
