  CoreEntry(
    id: 'isra-miraj',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\bisra\b',
      r'\bmiraj\b',
      r'\bmiraj\b',
      r'\bnight journey\b',
      r'\bascension\b',
      r'\bburaq\b',
      r'\bsidrat\b',
      r'\blote tree\b',
      r'\bwhy (?:do )?(?:we|muslims?) pray (?:five|5) times\b',
      r'\bwhere (?:did|do) the (?:five|5) prayers come from\b',
      r'\bwhen (?:was|did) the (?:five|5) prayers? (?:made|become) (?:obligatory|fard)\b',
    ],
    answer: 'The Isra is the night journey in which Allah took the Prophet ﷺ from the Sacred Mosque in Makkah to the Aqsa Mosque in Jerusalem: "Exalted is He who took His Servant by night from al-Masjid al-Haram to al-Masjid al-Aqsa, whose surroundings We have blessed, to show him of Our signs" (Qur\'an 17:1). The Mi\'raj is his ascension through the heavens to the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary, mentioned in Qur\'an 53:13-18.\n\nThe hadith adds details: he was carried on the Buraq, led the prophets in prayer in Jerusalem (Sahih Muslim 162), and he was ascended through the heavens. In the report in Bukhari, Allah first enjoined fifty daily prayers; on Musa\'s advice the Prophet ﷺ asked for a reduction until it became five, and Allah said these five are equal to fifty in reward (Sahih al-Bukhari 349).\n\nThis is why the five daily prayers are called a gift from the Mi\'raj. Scholars differ on the exact date of the night, and the Prophet ﷺ did not set a day for celebrating it, so there is no authentic date to mark.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 17:1, 53:13; Sahih Muslim 162; Sahih al-Bukhari 349.',
    topic: 'isra',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The date of the Isra and Mi\'raj is described as disputed; no authentic day is given. Flagged for scholar review.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'four-caliphs',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:four|4) (?:rightly guided )?caliphs?\b',
      r'\brightly guided caliphs?\b',
