    answer: 'Sunni and Shia Muslims share the core of Islam: they believe in one God, in Muhammad ﷺ as His final messenger (Qur\'an 33:40), in the Qur\'an, the angels, the Last Day, and they pray towards the same Ka\'bah, fast in Ramadan, give zakat, and perform Hajj.\n\nThe main historical difference is over who was entitled to lead the community after the Prophet ﷺ: Sunnis accept the leadership of Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and Ali in that order, while Shia hold that Ali and his descendants were the rightful leaders. From this follow differences in some hadith collections, in schools of law, and in some practices.\n\nI cannot say which group is "better", and this app does not rank Muslims. Allah tells believers to hold together and not divide: "And hold firmly to the rope of Allah all together and do not become divided" (Qur\'an 3:103), and "The believers are but brothers, so make settlement between your brothers" (Qur\'an 49:10). For questions of belief and practice, ask a scholar of the tradition you follow.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 33:40, 3:103, 49:10.\n[GO:/sources/policy]',
    topic: 'sects',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'The Sunni/Shia description is a neutral, minimal account; flagged for scholar review by both traditions if possible.',
  ),
