      r'\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:eid al adha|eid ul adha|bakra eid)\b',
      r'\bwhy (?:do )?(?:we|muslims?) celebrate eid al adha\b',
    ],
    answer: 'The Qur\'an tells that Ibrahim (Abraham) saw in a dream that he must sacrifice his son, and the son agreed: "indeed I have seen in a dream that I [must] sacrifice you, so see what you think" (Qur\'an 37:102). The son answered: "do as you are commanded. You will find me, if Allah wills, of the steadfast" (Qur\'an 37:102). When both had submitted, Allah called to him: "You have fulfilled the vision" (Qur\'an 37:105), and "We ransomed him with a great sacrifice" (Qur\'an 37:107), a ram in place of the son.\n\nIt was a trial of obedience and love, and shows that Allah does not want human life but obedience and sacrifice from the heart. The Qur\'an does not name the son in these verses; the majority of scholars say it was Ismail, and some say Ishaq.\n\nEid al-Adha remembers this. Muslims who can afford it offer a sacrifice of an animal (udhiyah/qurbani) and share the meat with family and the poor, and pilgrims complete the rites of Hajj. The sacrifice is itself an act of worship, not a payment for sins.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 37:102, 37:105, 37:107.',
