    answer: 'Muhammad ﷺ is the last Messenger of Allah ("Muhammad is not the father of [any] one of your men, but [he is] the Messenger of Allah and last of the prophets" (Qur\'an 33:40)). In outline:\n- He was born in Makkah, around 570 CE, in the tribe of Quraysh; he was orphaned young and was known among his people as the truthful and trustworthy (as-Sadiq al-Amin).\n- At the age of forty he began to receive the revelation. Ibn Abbas said the Prophet started receiving the Divine Inspiration at the age of forty, stayed in Makkah for thirteen years, was ordered to migrate, lived as an emigrant for ten years and died at sixty-three (Sahih al-Bukhari 3902). It began in the cave of Hira, with the first words of Surah Al-Alaq (Qur\'an 96:1): "Recite in the name of your Lord who created". Bukhari describes the beginning in Sahih al-Bukhari 3.\n- In Makkah he invited people to worship Allah alone, and many suffered for it. After the hardship at Ta\'if, when the people there did not respond, the angel of the mountains offered to crush them and he refused, hoping that Allah would bring from them people who worship Him alone (Sahih al-Bukhari 3231).\n- He migrated to Madinah (the Hijrah), built the first community there, and returned to Makkah in the conquest, a few years before his death.\n- He died in Madinah at the age of sixty-three (Sahih Muslim 2348). He left no wealth: Aisha said the Prophet died while his iron armor was mortgaged to a Jew for thirty sa of barley (Sahih al-Bukhari 2916).\n\nAllah describes him as "of a great moral character" (Qur\'an 68:4) and as "a mercy to the worlds" (Qur\'an 21:107). The Seerah section of the Academy has his life in detail. His image is never drawn in Islam, out of respect.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 33:40, 96:1, 68:4, 21:107; Sahih al-Bukhari 3902, 3, 3231, 2916; Sahih Muslim 2348.\n[GO:/academy/seerah]',
