      r'\bchina\b',
    ],
    answer: 'A hadith is a report of what the Prophet ﷺ said, did, or approved. Together these reports are his sunnah, the second source of Islamic teaching after the Qur\'an. Each report has a text and a chain of narrators (isnad) back to the Prophet ﷺ.\n\nThe scholars of hadith graded reports by the reliability of the chain and text:\n- Sahih (authentic): sound chain and text.\n- Hasan (good): slightly less strong, but still reliable.\n- Da\'if (weak): has a defect; not used as evidence for rulings, and may be mentioned only with its grade stated.\n- Mawdu\' (fabricated): invented; it must not be attributed to the Prophet ﷺ.\n\nThe six major collections are Sahih al-Bukhari, Sahih Muslim, Sunan Abu Dawud, Jami\' at-Tirmidhi, Sunan an-Nasa\'i and Sunan Ibn Majah. The first two are the most trusted, and a report found in both is called "agreed upon". The other four mix reports of different grades, so each report must be graded: this app shows the grade and the grader next to a hadith from those four. Look at the source card under an answer to see the collection, the number and the grade. For rulings, ask a qualified scholar.\n[GO:/hadith]\n[GO:/sources]',
    topic: 'hadith',
    beforeGate: true,
    reviewNote: 'Standard definitions; nothing cited from a specific report.',
  ),
  CoreEntry(
    id: 'quran-vs-hadith',
    patterns: <String>[
      r'\b(?:difference|different|distinction) between (?:the )?(?:quran|quran|koran) and (?:the )?hadiths?\b',
