    ],
    answer: 'The Qur\'an is the word of Allah, revealed in Arabic words and preserved letter by letter. A hadith is a report of the words and actions of the Prophet ﷺ, passed on by narrators, and is graded for its reliability. Both are sources of guidance, and Muslims are told to take both.\n\nThe Qur\'an itself says to follow the Messenger: "And whatever the Messenger has given you - take; and what he has forbidden you - refrain from" (Qur\'an 59:7), "He who obeys the Messenger has obeyed Allah" (Qur\'an 4:80), and that he is there to explain it: "And We revealed to you the message that you may make clear to the people what was sent down to them" (Qur\'an 16:44). The details of prayer, zakat and Hajj, for example, are known mainly through his practice.\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ also warned against dropping this: Al-Miqdam ibn Ma\'dikarib narrated the Prophet said I have been given the Qur\'an and something like it, yet the time is coming when a man replete on his couch will say keep to the Qur\'an, what you find in it permissible treat as permissible, and what you find prohibited treat as prohibited (Sunan Abu Dawud 4604, sahih per al-Albani).\n\nBecause hadiths vary in strength, always check the grade; a weak or invented report is not evidence. Use the source card in this app.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 59:7, 4:80, 16:44; Sunan Abu Dawud 4604, sahih per al-Albani.',
