    answer: 'Allah is far more loving and merciful than we imagine, and He wants you to come back to Him. In a hard time He comforted His Prophet ﷺ: "Your Lord has not taken leave of you" (Qur\'an 93:3), and His mercy is open to every servant who turns to Him.\n\nHe describes Himself as "the Forgiving, the Affectionate" (Qur\'an 85:14), and says "My mercy encompasses all things" (Qur\'an 7:156). The Prophet ﷺ once saw a mother who found her child among the captives and held him to her chest, and asked his companions if they thought she would throw her child in the fire. They said no. The Prophet said Allah is more merciful to His slaves than this lady is to her son (Sahih al-Bukhari 5999).\n\nIf you have sinned, even greatly, the door is open: "do not despair of the mercy of Allah" (Qur\'an 39:53). Repentance (tawbah) is to stop the sin, feel regret, ask Allah to forgive you, resolve not to return, and, if you wronged a person, put the wrong right. Allah loves this: "Indeed, Allah loves those who are constantly repentant and loves those who purify themselves" (Qur\'an 2:222). Start by praying, making du\'a in your own words, and doing a small good deed today. If you are feeling lost, hopeless or in pain, please also talk with someone you trust.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 93:3, 85:14, 7:156, 39:53, 2:222; Sahih al-Bukhari 5999.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-tawba]',
