    answer: 'Islam\'s teaching is to protect life and to be just to everyone. The Qur\'an says that killing an innocent person is like killing all of mankind: "whoever kills a soul unless for a soul or for corruption [done] in the land - it is as if he had slain mankind entirely. And whoever saves one - it is as if he had saved mankind entirely" (Qur\'an 5:32). Terror against civilians is a major sin and has no place in Islam, whoever does it and in whatever name.\n\nJihad means "striving". The greater part of it is the struggle against one\'s own desires and for good conduct. Fighting is permitted only in limited cases of defence against wrongs, with firm limits: "Fight in the way of Allah those who fight you but do not transgress. Indeed. Allah does not like transgressors" (Qur\'an 2:190), "Permission [to fight] has been given to those who are being fought, because they were wronged" (Qur\'an 22:39). The Prophet ﷺ forbade the killing of women and children (Sahih al-Bukhari 3015), and warned against harming people under a treaty (Sahih al-Bukhari 3166).\n\nKindness to people of other faiths who do not fight you is commanded: "Allah does not forbid you from those who do not fight you because of religion and do not expel you from your homes - from being righteous toward them and acting justly toward them" (Qur\'an 60:8), and "There shall be no compulsion in [acceptance of] the religion" (Qur\'an 2:256). Whether any particular war is lawful is a question for qualified scholars, not for individuals or groups.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 5:32, 2:190, 22:39, 60:8, 2:256; Sahih al-Bukhari 3015, 3166.',
