    answer: 'I answer from Islam, so I can tell you what Muslims believe, and I will not mock or attack anyone\'s faith.\n\nMuslims believe that Allah sent messengers to every people with one message, to worship Him alone, and that Muhammad ﷺ is the last of them. So Muslims say that the religion with Allah is Islam, meaning submission to Him ("Indeed, the religion in the sight of Allah is Islam" (Qur\'an 3:19)), the way of Ibrahim: "Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian, but he was one inclining toward truth, a Muslim [submitting to Allah]" (Qur\'an 3:67).\n\nOn Jesus (Isa): Muslims honour him as one of the greatest messengers, born miraculously to Maryam, and a word from Allah. The Qur\'an says: "The Messiah, Jesus, the son of Mary, was but a messenger of Allah and His word which He directed to Mary and a soul [created at a command] from Him" (Qur\'an 4:171), and teaches that he is not God or the son of God, and rejects the Trinity, and says that to claim Allah is the third of three is disbelief: "And there is no god except one God" (Qur\'an 5:73). The Qur\'an also compares his creation to that of Adam (Qur\'an 3:59). So Muslims and Christians agree on his honour and miracles, and differ on who he is.\n\nHow Muslims are told to treat others: "There shall be no compulsion in [acceptance of] the religion" (Qur\'an 2:256), "do not argue with the People of the Scripture except in a way that is best" (Qur\'an 29:46), and in the last words of Surah Al-Kafirun: "For you is your religion, and for me is my religion" (Qur\'an 109:6). Whom Allah admits to Paradise is for Him to judge; a Muslim\'s duty is to invite with kindness and wisdom and to be just to everyone.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 3:19, 3:67, 4:171, 5:73, 3:59, 2:256, 29:46, 109:6.',
